---
layout: layouts/base.njk
title: Join the Order
permalink: /join/
description: "Create an EGregoRA account or sign in — a verified email address, and then your face or fingerprint. No passwords, anywhere."
---

<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">The Threshold</p>
  <h1>Join the Order</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">No passwords. An address you can prove is yours, and after that your own face or
  fingerprint if your device has one. Membership costs nothing, confers no rank, and withholds no teaching —
  everything on this site stays public. An account simply means the oracle knows who it is talking to.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal" id="gate">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <div class="auth-box" id="auth-box" data-state="start">

      <!-- ------------------------------------------------ signed out --- -->
      <div class="auth-pane" data-pane="start">
        <p class="kicker">STEP ONE · YOUR ADDRESS</p>
        <h2>Sign in, or create an account</h2>
        <p class="muted small">One form does both. If the address is new, an account is made; if it is known,
        you are signed back in. Either way a six-digit code goes to the inbox and nothing is created until
        you enter it.</p>

        <form class="ornate" id="auth-start-form">
          <div>
            <label for="a-email">Email address</label>
            <input id="a-email" type="email" name="email" required autocomplete="email"
                   inputmode="email" placeholder="rowan@example.org">
          </div>
          <div>
            <label for="a-name">Name <span class="muted xsmall">(optional — what the oracle should call you)</span></label>
            <input id="a-name" type="text" name="name" maxlength="80" autocomplete="name"
                   placeholder="Rowan of the North">
          </div>
          <div class="ask-actions">
            <button class="btn" type="submit" id="a-send">Send my code</button>
            <button class="btn btn--ghost" type="button" id="a-bio" hidden>
              <span aria-hidden="true">☉</span> Use face or fingerprint
            </button>
          </div>
        </form>
        <p class="auth-msg" id="a-msg" role="status"></p>
      </div>

      <!-- -------------------------------------------------- the code --- -->
      <div class="auth-pane" data-pane="code" hidden>
        <p class="kicker">STEP TWO · THE SIX DIGITS</p>
        <h2>Check your inbox</h2>
        <p class="muted small" id="a-code-note">A code is on its way. It lasts fifteen minutes and works once.</p>
        <form class="ornate" id="auth-code-form">
          <div>
            <label for="a-code">Verification code</label>
            <input id="a-code" type="text" name="code" required inputmode="numeric" pattern="[0-9]*"
                   maxlength="6" autocomplete="one-time-code" class="code-input" placeholder="000000">
          </div>
          <div class="ask-actions">
            <button class="btn" type="submit" id="a-check">Verify and enter</button>
            <button class="btn btn--ghost" type="button" id="a-again">Send another</button>
            <button class="btn btn--ghost" type="button" id="a-back">Different address</button>
          </div>
        </form>
        <p class="auth-msg" id="a-code-msg" role="status"></p>
      </div>

      <!-- --------------------------------------------------- signed in -- -->
      <div class="auth-pane" data-pane="in" hidden>
        <p class="kicker">INSIDE</p>
        <h2 id="a-greeting">Welcome</h2>
        <p class="muted small" id="a-who"></p>

        <div class="auth-bio">
          <h3>Biometric entry</h3>
          <p class="muted small">Add a passkey and your device will let you back in with
          <strong>Face ID, Touch ID, Windows Hello, an Android fingerprint</strong> or a hardware key. The
          scan happens on your device and stays there: nothing biometric is sent here, ever. All this server
          keeps is a public key and the device's word — cryptographically signed — that it checked a real
          human.</p>
          <ul class="passkey-list" id="a-keys"></ul>
          <p class="ask-actions">
            <button class="btn" type="button" id="a-add-key">Enrol this device</button>
            <button class="btn btn--ghost" type="button" id="a-signout">Sign out</button>
          </p>
          <p class="auth-msg" id="a-bio-msg" role="status"></p>
          <p class="muted xsmall" id="a-bio-unsupported" hidden>This browser or device does not offer
          platform biometrics, so the emailed code remains your way in. Nothing is lost by that.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h3>What an account is for</h3>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li>The oracle greets you by name and keeps the thread of what you have asked.</li>
        <li>Files you hand it stay attached to you rather than to a browser tab.</li>
        <li>Images and clips you make are gathered in one place instead of scattering.</li>
        <li>Nothing on this site is hidden behind it. There are no grades, no fees and no secrets withheld
        from the page — an account is a convenience, not an initiation.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h3>How the door is built</h3>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> <strong>No passwords.</strong> Nothing to reuse, leak, phish or reset.
        Proof of the address is a one-time code that expires in fifteen minutes.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> <strong>Passkeys, not fingerprints.</strong> WebAuthn keeps the
        biometric on your device's secure element; the server only ever sees a public key and a signature.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> <strong>User verification required.</strong> We reject any sign-in
        where the device did not actually check a face, a finger or a PIN.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> <strong>Sessions expire.</strong> Thirty days, revocable by signing
        out, and every passkey can be forgotten individually.</li>
        <li><span class="myth">◆</span> We will not sell the list, because there is nothing we want to sell
        it for.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
